texts:
User.findAll().forEach(user -> {
    List<Order> orders = user.getOrders(); // lazy load fires HERE
    orders.forEach(this::process);
});

looks innocent. `getOrders()` is a getter. but with lazy loading, each call executes `SELECT * FROM orders WHERE user_id = ?`. run it for 50 users, you get 51 queries. the N+1 hides behind clean syntax.

fix: fetch join or entity graph.

@Query("select u from User u left join fetch u.orders")
List<User> findAllWithOrders();

or

@EntityGraph(attributePaths = "orders")
List<User> findAll();

re-run with logging on. one query. `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` shows a single hash join instead of fifty index scans. same ORM. different pattern. measured, not assumed.

The generated SQL before the fix:
```
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE user_id = 42 LIMIT 20;
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE id = 101;
SELECT * FROM addresses WHERE customer_id = 101;
-- repeated 20 times
```
The ORM executed each `SELECT` separately. No joins. No batching. Just silent N+1 death by a thousand cuts.

Generated SQL before the fix:  
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE user_id = 123;  
Then fifty times: SELECT * FROM items WHERE order_id = ?;  
The database does the work. The network pays the latency. The ORM logs show nothing suspicious unless you filter by query count.

The query log tells the truth:

```
SELECT * FROM users LIMIT 100;
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE user_id = 1;
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE user_id = 2;
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE user_id = 3;
-- 97 more identical queries
```

One hundred users. One hundred one queries. The N+1 is not a theory. It is in your logs right now.
